\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Weighted graph differentials and adjoints}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring, #{V,E} finite sets and
#{s,h:E\to V} endpoint maps. The
\vocabk{ring-valued incidence map}{fdip-0010} is
#{d_R:H_V\to H_E}, where #{H_V=\mathcal F_R(V)},
#{H_E=\mathcal F_R(E)} and #{d_Rf(e)=f(h(e))-f(s(e))}.
Equip both modules with the symmetric counting forms
##{b_V(f,g)=\sum_v f(v)g(v),\qquad b_E(a,c)=\sum_e a(e)c(e).}
Their \vocabk{perfect counting dualities}{fdip-001F} give the
\newvocab{counting adjoint} #{d_R^\dagger:H_E\to H_V} by
\ref{fdip-001P}. It is characterized by
#{b_E(d_Rf,a)=b_V(f,d_R^\dagger a)}.}

\p{The return map can be read directly from the endpoints:
##{(d_R^\dagger a)(v)
 =\sum_{h(e)=v}a(e)-\sum_{s(e)=v}a(e).}}
\proof{
\p{Expanding and regrouping finite sums gives
##{\begin{aligned}
b_E(d_Rf,a)
 &=\sum_e\bigl(f(h(e))-f(s(e))\bigr)a(e)\\
 &=\sum_v f(v)\left(\sum_{h(e)=v}a(e)-\sum_{s(e)=v}a(e)\right).
\end{aligned}}
This is the characterizing identity, so uniqueness identifies the map.
Each loop occurs once in both sums and cancels; parallel edges remain
separate summands. If #{V} is empty, existence of #{s:E\to V} forces
#{E} empty; the formula gives the unique map of zero modules.}
}

\p{For arbitrary weights #{w:E\to R}, let
#{W:H_E\to H_E} multiply pointwise by #{w}:
#{(Wa)(e)=w(e)a(e)}. The \newvocab{weighted graph Laplacian} is
##{L=d_R^\dagger Wd_R:H_V\longrightarrow H_V.}
Commutativity makes #{W} self-adjoint, since
#{b_E(Wa,c)=\sum_e w(e)a(e)c(e)=b_E(a,Wc)}.
The weighted edge form
##{b_{E,w}(a,c)=b_E(a,Wc)=\sum_e w(e)a(e)c(e)}
is symmetric bilinear. It is perfect if and only if every #{w(e)}
is a unit, meaning an invertible element of #{R}.}

\proof{
\p{Its duality map is #{\Phi_EW}. Since #{\Phi_E} is an
isomorphism, #{\Phi_EW} is an isomorphism exactly when #{W} is.
If every weight is a unit, multiplication by #{w(e)^{-1}} gives both
inverse identities pointwise. Conversely, if #{WJ=I}, evaluate
#{WJ\delta_e=\delta_e} at #{e}:
##{w(e)(J\delta_e)(e)=1.}
Commutativity supplies the other inverse identity for this scalar, so
#{w(e)} is a unit. For #{E=\varnothing}, the unit condition is vacuous
and #{W} is the unique isomorphism of the zero module. In the zero ring,
#{0=1} is itself a unit, consistent with the same proof.}
}

\p{Even when some weights are nonunits, the represented return map
#{d_R^\dagger W} exists and satisfies
##{b_{E,w}(d_Rf,a)=b_V(f,d_R^\dagger Wa).}
Its existence does not make the weighted edge form perfect. Thus it
does not by itself give a two-sided adjoint involution for that form.
All these ring identities are direct deductions from the chosen
counting dualities. Their positive-real incidence convention is
\citet{Section 2.1, p. 4}{spielman2009effective}.}

\p{Orientation reversal leaves #{L} unchanged. Choose which edges to
reverse, and put #{\varepsilon(e)=-1} on those edges and #{1} on the
others, interpreted in #{R}. The sign map #{Ja(e)=\varepsilon(e)a(e)}
satisfies #{J^\dagger=J}, #{J^2=I}, #{JW=WJ}, and the reversed
incidence map is #{d'_R=Jd_R}. Therefore
##{(d'_R)^\dagger Wd'_R=d_R^\dagger JWJd_R=d_R^\dagger Wd_R.}
The corresponding edge field is #{a'=Ja}, and
#{b_{E,w}(Ja,Jc)=b_{E,w}(a,c)}. The proof includes characteristic two,
loops and parallel edges. Endpoints and weights are otherwise fixed.}

\p{For the real incidence adapter, start with the
\vocabk{incidence map}{fdip-0002}
#{D:\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R^E} and positive weights #{w(e)>0}.
The counting forms are the \vocabk{counting inner products}{fdip-0002},
and write #{D^*=d_{\mathbb R}^\dagger}. The edge form becomes the
\newvocab{weighted inner product}
##{\langle a,c\rangle_{E,w}=\sum_e w(e)a(e)c(e).}
It is symmetric bilinear, and its self-pairing is a finite sum of
nonnegative terms. Strictly positive weights make that sum zero only
at the zero edge field. Keeping the vertex pairing counting, the
\newvocab{weighted adjoint} is
#{D_w^\dagger=D^*W}, since
##{\langle Df,a\rangle_{E,w}
 =\langle Df,Wa\rangle_E=\langle f,D^*Wa\rangle_V.}
Hence #{L=D^*WD=D_w^\dagger D}; #{D^*D} is the unit-weight case.}

\p{For the coordinate recovery, use the ordered-value isomorphisms
#{c_V,c_E} and \vocabk{incidence matrix}{fdip-0003} #{B=c_EDc_V^{-1}}.
The endpoint return formula gives
#{c_VD^*c_E^{-1}=B^T}. The multiplication map #{W} has diagonal
array #{\operatorname{diag}(w(e))}; writing the same letter for that
array yields #{[D_w^\dagger]=B^TW} and #{[L]=B^TWB}.
For a single edge #{0\to1} of weight #{c>0}, the two adjoints send
an edge value #{a} to #{(-a,a)} and #{(-ca,ca)}, respectively.
Under orientation reversal the sign array #{S} gives #{B'=SB},
#{S^T=S}, #{S^2=I}, #{SWS=W}, and
#{(B')^TWB'=B^TWB}. A loop has a zero incidence row before and
after reversal.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.toMatrix_adjoint} identifies adjoint
matrices with conjugate transposes in orthonormal bases. Over the reals
this is transpose. The coordinate edge basis is orthonormal for counting,
but not generally for the weighted pairing; the return-map calculation
above accounts for the extra #{W}.}
